  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA low pressure system currently off the coast of the Pacific \nNorthwest will lead to a greater troughing pattern over the West \nCoast into the weekend. Today will be dry with scattered clouds and \ntemperatures near to slightly below average. As the low pressure \nsystem moves southward, an open wave trough will begin to form \nbringing in greater moisture from the southwest. PWAT values rise \nnear one inch at San Diego. The marine layer will slowly become \nsaturated tonight into Saturday, where hi-res models continue to \nshow areas of light showers from the coastal slopes of the mountains \nto the coast. Since these seem to be isolated in nature, the \nforecast shows around 15-25% chance of rain for these areas \nThursday night through Friday night. \n\nA large area of moisture in the form of a subtropical low pressure \nsystem that we continue to monitor is slowly forming well off the\ncoast. Models are beginning to agree on a more southerly track\nwith the main swath of the heaviest rains going into Baja\nCalifornia, where IVT values near 300 kg/m/s (weaker values) for\nSoCal. Nonetheless, bouts of moderate to heavier rain are \nexpected Saturday night into Sunday morning. With the model \nguidance of a more southerly track of the system, confidence \nremains high on San Diego County seeing the most precipitation \noverall, as well as all coastal mountain slopes. Confidence is not\nas high on rain totals further north, where there is a larger \nmodel spread. PWAT will peak near 1.5 inches in San Diego, as the \nsubtropical low and low to the north pass through, so sufficient\nmoisture will be in place. \n\nThe interactions between the subtropical low and the low pressure\nsystem to the north is still somewhat uncertain, where exact rain\ntotals are still in question. Chances for 1 inch of rain in total\nover the weekend has stayed pretty consistent across San Diego \nCounty (50-60%), closer to 75% in the mountains. These chances \ndecrease across the LA basin near 35-45%. Chances for one half \ninch in this area increases to 55-65%, 35-50% chance across desert\nregions. Some heavier bands of showers may occur over San Diego \ncounty closer to the core of the subtropical low, which may bring \ngreater amounts locally. In terms of timing, light to moderate\nshowers will push in from west to east sometime on Saturday\nafternoon into the evening. The period for the heaviest rainfall \nwill be later on Saturday night into early Sunday morning. \nRainfall will become more scattered about late Sunday morning \nthrough early evening, with peaks of sunshine starting to make a \nreturn in parts of the deserts and western LA basin as the system \nmoves eastward.\n\nDry and sunnier weather is expected by Monday as weak high pressure \nmoves over the area. This will bring a subtle warming trend, though \nkeeping temperatures near normal readings for early November. This \nweather looks to continue into at least Wednesday. Another low \npressure system may form somewhere off the California coast into the \nlatter half of next week. This may bring a slight chance of light\nshowers near the coast, though most of the region looks to stay \ndry for the rest of the week.",
